---
import { extensionFamilies, extensionFamily } from "../../guide/extensions/families.ts";
import GuidePage from "../../guide/GuidePage.astro";
import Path from "../../guide/Path.astro";
import Task from "../../guide/Task.astro";
import { sitePath } from "../../site/links.ts";

const tachiyomi = extensionFamily("tachiyomi");
const exampleLibrary = tachiyomi.versions.at(-1);
const lnreader = extensionFamily("lnreader");
---

<GuidePage slug="extension-compatibility" lead="Which extensions this version of Katari loads, and what a label under Not loaded means.">
  <Task id="versions" title="Which extensions Katari loads">
    <p>
      Katari comes with no extensions; they come from the stores you add, as in
      <a href={sitePath("docs/first-steps/#extension-store")}>First steps</a>. An extension loads when it was built with one of these
      library versions:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Extensions</th> <th>Built with</th> <th>Versions</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {extensionFamilies.map((family) => (
          <tr><td>{family.name}</td> <td>{family.library}</td> <td>{family.versions.join(", ")}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      An extension's version usually starts with its library version, so {exampleLibrary}.12 means {tachiyomi.library} {exampleLibrary}.
      {lnreader.name} are numbered on their own, so Katari takes their library version from their store's address.
      <Path>Browse › Extensions › ⋮ › Extension compatibility</Path> shows this list for the Katari you have installed.
    </p>
  </Task>

  <Task id="not-loaded" title="Why an extension isn't loaded">
    <p>Under <Path>Browse › Extensions</Path>, extensions that can't be used are listed under <strong>Not loaded</strong>:</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Shown as</th> <th>What to do</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Unsupported SDK</strong></td>
          <td>
            It was built with a library version not in the list above. Tap it: when it's older, update the extension from its store; when
            it's newer, a newer Katari may load it.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Torrents only</strong></td> <td>It finds episodes only as torrents, which Katari can't play. Use another extension.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Untrusted</strong></td> <td>Tap the shield and <strong>Trust</strong> if you trust where it came from.</td></tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Damaged</strong></td>
          <td>An extension from a store that doesn't sign them changed since it was installed, so it isn't run. Tap it and <strong>Reinstall</strong>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Filtered</strong></td> <td>Its content warning is one you didn't allow. Change <strong>Allowed content warnings</strong> under <Path>Settings › Browse</Path>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Conflict</strong></td> <td>Another installed extension provides the same sources. See <a href={sitePath("docs/troubleshooting/#extensions")}>Troubleshooting</a>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>No label</td> <td>Tap the info button for the reason. Usually updating the extension or Katari helps.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      A store's extensions with <strong>Unsupported SDK</strong> or <strong>Torrents only</strong> are listed with the same label, but can't
      be installed.
    </p>
  </Task>

  <Task id="not-signed" title="Stores that don't sign their extensions">
    <p>
      Stores of {lnreader.name} don't sign what they offer, so adding one asks first. What you install from such a store is labelled
      <strong>Not signed</strong>:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Katari runs it in a sandbox with time and memory limits, and checks its file against the one you installed before running it.
        Its info screen shows the file's SHA-256.
      </li>
      <li>
        It can still use your logins on sites you opened in Katari's <strong>WebView</strong>, and send what you type into its settings
        anywhere. Only add stores you trust.
      </li>
      <li>
        Its updates come only from the store you installed it from. Installing another store's copy asks before replacing it, and keeps
        your library, settings and logins.
      </li>
      <li>
        Its content warning is <strong>Not rated</strong>, since the store doesn't say whether it has 18+ content. Not rated is allowed
        at first; turn it off under <Path>Settings › Browse › Allowed content warnings</Path> to hide them from the store's list.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Task>
</GuidePage>
